    "q-133291": {
      "dom_id": "question-133291",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Find $y(t),$ the antiderivative of ${y}^{′}(t).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $y(t)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$+\\,C$",
      "worked_solution": "We know that ${y}^{′}(t)={t}^{2}-2.$\n\nApplying the sum rule, we compute the sum of the integrals of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {t}^{2}-2\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int {t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t-\\int 2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int {t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t-2\\int {t}^{0}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{{t}^{2+1}}{2+1})-2t+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}-2t+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "blank",
          "dom_id": "",
