    "q-133292": {
      "dom_id": "question-133292",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "The eagle's total displacement in the $y$-direction over the first $6$ seconds is given by ${\\int }_{0}^{6}{y}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.$ Calculate this displacement.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The eagle's total displacement in the $y$-direction over the first $6$ seconds is$\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,\\text{m}.$",
      "worked_solution": "In part 7, we found that the antiderivative of ${y}^{′}(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}-2t+C.\n$$\n\nBy the fundamental theorem of calculus, we take the antiderivative and evaluate the difference at the bounds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{6}{y}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =y(t){|}_{0}^{6} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}-2t)|}_{0}^{6} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}(6)^{3}-2(6))-(\\frac{1}{3}(0)^{3}-2(0)) \\\\  & =60-0 \\\\  & =60.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe eagle's total displacement in the $y$-direction over the first $6$ seconds is $60\\,\\text{m}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
