    "q-133294": {
      "dom_id": "question-133294",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "${r}^{′}(\\sqrt{5})=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $⟨$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$⟩$",
      "worked_solution": "We differentiate each component separately.\n\n- For the $x$-component, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{5}{25+{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand therefore,\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\sqrt{5})=\\frac{5}{25+(\\sqrt{5})^{2}}=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\n- For the $y$-component, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(t)={t}^{2}-2,\n$$\n\nand therefore,\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(\\sqrt{5})=(\\sqrt{5})^{2}-2=3.\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\sqrt{5}) & =⟨{x}^{′}(\\sqrt{5}),{y}^{′}(\\sqrt{5})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{6},3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
